{"items":[{"tags":["python","attr","python-attrs"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":76771965,"is_answered":false,"question_id":76765395,"item_type":"answer","score":2,"last_activity_date":1690379155,"creation_date":1690379155,"body":"I&#39;m a maintainer of attrs.\nI gather you&#39;re using the  Mypy option. A small problem here is that  isn&#39;t really a generic class, but the Mypy attrs plugin treats it as such because it&#39;s more useful that way. I think we&#39;ll change this in the future so it&#39;s generic in attrs too.\nBut in the meantime, what you want to do is using a string annotation instead. Here:\n\nI&#39;ve switched to the new  namespace and cleaned up the example a little. Note that the type of the  parameter,  is now generic ( vs just a bare ), and the type annotation is in quotes (i.e. a string).\nAnother solution would be to use  but that&#39;s a bigger change, so I would use the first.\n","excerpt":"A small problem here is that <span class=\"highlight\">attrs.Attribute</span> isn&#39;t really a generic class, but the Mypy attrs plugin treats it as such because it&#39;s more useful that way. &hellip; Note that the type of the attribute parameter, <span class=\"highlight\">attrs.Attribute</span>[str] is now generic (Attribute[str] vs just a bare Attribute), and the type annotation is in quotes (i.e. a string). &hellip; ","title":"How to mypy typehint an attrs validator attribute"},{"tags":["c#","json","dynamic"],"question_score":-1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":true,"question_id":59666317,"item_type":"question","score":-1,"last_activity_date":1579038468,"creation_date":1578580634,"body":"I could use some help looping through the following JSon:\n\n\n\nI deserialize the JSon to a dynamic:\n\n\n\nHere the code that I went with in the end:\n\n\n\nI didn&#39;t elect to go with this code, but if you need to limit the selection to the &quot;DataFields&quot; this code will work:\n\n\n\ntestrequestresponseinfocs.p_TestResult.Substring(Convert.ToInt16(acoplasttighteningresultdatacs.p_AttributeIDStartPosition) - 1,\n                                                                                                 Convert.ToInt16(acoplasttighteningresultdatacs.p_AttributeIDLength));\n              #endregion\n              acoplasttighteningresultdatacs.p_AttributeValueStartPosition = attrs[3];\n              acoplasttighteningresultdatacs.p_AttributeValueStartLength = attrs[4];\n              #region &quot;Load attribute values from controller response mesage&quot;\n               acoplasttighteningresultdatacs.p_AttributeValue =\ntestrequestresponseinfocs.p_TestResult.Substring(Convert.ToInt16(acoplasttighteningresultdatacs.p_AttributeValueStartPosition) - 1,\n                                                                                                 Convert.ToInt16(acoplasttighteningresultdatacs.p_AttributeValueStartLength));\n              #endregion\n\n\n\nNeeded to retrieve the attribute information for the business rules; so, I loaded the last tightening result data repository into a dictionary, which allowed easy look of the attribute info.  This way if the schema info changes the solution will still be able to find the attribute info.  (I.E. TighteningStatus).  It will find the last tightening result data for all the business rule attributes and all have to pass.\n\n\n\nHere&#39;s the load dictionary for repository code:\n\n\n\nHere is the get attribute id from dictionary method.  I load it to a static generic list, so that I can access the values on the UI thread:\n\n\n","excerpt":"[0];                                    \n            acoplasttighteningresultdatacs.p_AttributeIDStartPosition = <span class=\"highlight\">attrs.Attribute</span>[1];                                    \n            acoplasttighteningresultdatacs.p_AttributeIDLength &hellip; = <span class=\"highlight\">attrs.Attribute</span>[2];\n            if (attrs.LastTighteningResultDataRev3 == &quot;DataFields&quot;) acoplasttighteningresultdatacs.p_AttributeValueStartPosition = <span class=\"highlight\">attrs.Attribute</span>[3];\n            if (attrs.LastTighteningResultDataRev3 &hellip; ","title":"C# Parse Json Dynamic Object"},{"tags":["javascript","angularjs","angularjs-directive"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":37080495,"item_type":"question","score":2,"last_activity_date":1462608149,"creation_date":1462564859,"body":"I am relatively new to Angular and this my first attempt at using a custom directive. I have a table I am working with and each cell has a data attribute. For example, &quot;data-dept=&quot;service&quot;, and I would like to access this value before overwriting it with a directive. In my directive, I have set template = true, and this removes my data attribute. \n\nI liked my website that I am working to better explain what I am attempting to do.\nhttp://partnerportal-preprod.automate-webservices.com/list/#/hoursTable\n\nThe cells inside the table in the first row are clickable, but I would like to know if the user is clicking on service for example.\n\nUpdate\n\nI created plunker to better illustrate my problem.\nI have a basic table that I am using a directive to replace a row in the table.\nIn doing so, I lose the attribute that I would need to set it as a key in my object for later use.\n\nhttp://plnkr.co/edit/oXRM6lRkidnAHfBA4GsR?p=preview\n\nHTML\n\n\n\nDirective\n\n\n","excerpt":"transclude: &#39;true&#39;,\n                    scope: {\n                      position: &#39;=&#39;\n                    },\n             link: function (scope,element,attrs,ngModel) { \n\n\n                console.log(<span class=\"highlight\">attrs.attribute</span> &hellip; ","title":"get an element attributes values using Angular after using template"},{"tags":["javascript","angularjs"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":27798994,"item_type":"question","score":1,"last_activity_date":1420548662,"creation_date":1420548404,"body":"My directive is the following\n\n\n\nAnd I use the directive as \n\n\n\nHowever the  value in the link function resolves to the string , instead of being an array. \n\nI will appreciate any kind of help or guidance. \n\nThanks!\n","excerpt":"attributes: &#39;=&#39;,\n            },\n            replace : true\n        }\n    });\n\n\nAnd I use the directive as \n\n&lt;my-table attributes=&quot;management.table.attributes&quot;&gt;&lt;/my-table&gt;\n\n\nHowever the <span class=\"highlight\">attrs.attribute</span> &hellip; ","title":"Angular Directive Interpreting attribute as string"},{"tags":["javascript","angularjs"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":27799058,"is_answered":false,"question_id":27798994,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1420548657,"creation_date":1420548657,"body":" is always going to be a string, because attributes are strings by definition. You need corresponding  which is going to be evaluated object reference:\n\n\n","excerpt":"<span class=\"highlight\">attrs.attribute</span> is always going to be a string, because attributes are strings by definition. &hellip; ","title":"Angular Directive Interpreting attribute as string"}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":224}